.mutation-backups/

# --- Markdown / documentation (untracked 2026-08-05) ---
# Files remain on disk; git simply stops tracking them.
*.md
*.mdx

# Two exceptions, tracked on purpose.
#
# ORDER MATTERS AND IS THE WHOLE POINT: the LAST matching pattern wins, so a negation
# is only alive until something broader re-matches below it. `!.env.example` (line 4)
# is undone by `.env*` (line 41); .env.example survives purely because it is already
# tracked, and .gitignore does not apply to tracked files. Untrack it once and it
# would vanish. These two negations sit below `*.md` with nothing broader after them.
# Verify with `git check-ignore -v --no-index <path>` — WITHOUT --no-index git hides
# the match for anything tracked, which is exactly how this stays invisible.
#
# Why these two and not the rest: CLAUDE.md is the instruction file every session
# loads, and LEARNINGS.md is the running record a PR is supposed to append to. Both
# were silently dropped by the 2026-08-08 squash, so a fresh worktree cut from
# wt-web had neither — sessions ran without the project's own rules, and the
# "one LEARNINGS.md line per PR" step had no file to write to.
# Leading slash = repo root ONLY. Unanchored, `!CLAUDE.md` would also un-ignore the
# eight per-package CLAUDE.md files, which this change is not restoring — quietly
# widening an ignore rule beyond what was asked is how the next surprise gets made.
!/CLAUDE.md
!/LEARNINGS.md

# --- Restored 2026-08-08: the rest of what the squash dropped ---
#
# Same rule as above and the same reason for the leading slashes: every pattern here is
# ANCHORED to the repo root, so none of them can match a same-named file somewhere else
# in the tree. `!/apps/*/CLAUDE.md` un-ignores exactly the per-package instruction files
# and not, say, a CLAUDE.md vendored inside a dependency; `!/docs/**/*.md` cannot reach a
# `docs/` folder belonging to some package.
#
# `**/` matches ZERO or more directories, so /docs/**/*.md covers both docs/00_README.md
# and docs/design2.0/UI_RULES_v3.md. Verified with `git check-ignore -v --no-index`.
#
# NOTHING BROADER MAY BE ADDED BELOW THIS BLOCK. A later `*.md`, or any pattern that
# re-matches these paths, silently re-ignores them — and because git suppresses
# check-ignore output for tracked files, the breakage would only surface the next time
# someone cut a fresh worktree. That is precisely how these files went missing.
#
# `.claude/settings.local.json` (line 13) and `.claude/worktrees/` (line 10) are NOT
# un-ignored here, and must not be: the first is per-user, the second is scratch.
!/docs/**/*.md
!/docs/**/*.mdx
!/apps/*/CLAUDE.md
!/packages/*/CLAUDE.md
!/.claude/agents/**/*.md
!/.claude/commands/**/*.md
!/.claude/skills/**/*.md
!/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug.md

# apps/web/REQUESTS.md — the cross-lane request log. TRACKED SINCE BEFORE THIS LINE
# EXISTED, and until 2026-08-12 only because it was already tracked: `*.md` (line 51)
# matched it and no negation below reached it, so `git check-ignore -v` on a tracked
# file printed nothing and the hole stayed invisible. Untracking it once would have
# deleted it from every fresh worktree, exactly as the 2026-08-08 squash did to
# CLAUDE.md and LEARNINGS.md. Verify with --no-index or you are verifying nothing.
#
# NARROW ON PURPOSE — one file, not `!/apps/*/REQUESTS.md`. `apps/jobs/REQUESTS.md`
# and `packages/billing/REQUESTS.md` also exist on disk and have NEVER been tracked;
# a class rule would un-ignore both, and the next `git add -A` would commit two files
# nobody reviewed. Widening later is one line. Reported rather than taken — the same
# rule the CLAUDE.md block above states and then follows.
!/apps/web/REQUESTS.md

# Playwright storageState for local visual verification — never commit a session.
.auth/

# Local visual-verification scratch: rendered fragments + screenshots.
# Generated by the *.harness.test.tsx files; never source, never committed.
.ui-port-shots/

# The gate runner's per-stage logs and verdict. Machine output, not source.
.gate/

# The status page is BUILT by .github/workflows/status-page.yml from live probes.
# Committing a copy would publish a measurement taken at commit time and let it
# read as current — the exact staleness the page exists to make visible.
public/status/
